\section{The \code{save} event}{


\code{save} is a core module that \code{simInit()} adds for you -- you never write it.
Its job is to run \code{\link[=saveFiles]{saveFiles()}} at the right times:

\tabular{ll}{
\code{init} \tab Creates \code{outputPath} if needed. If \code{\link[=outputs]{outputs()}} has any rows,
schedules the first \code{save.spades} event at the earliest \code{saveTime}, using
that row's \code{eventPriority} (default \code{.last()}).\cr
\code{spades}, \code{later} \tab Call \code{\link[=saveFiles]{saveFiles()}}, which writes every object whose
\code{saveTime} is the current time, then schedules the next one.\cr
\code{end} \tab Calls \code{\link[=saveFiles]{saveFiles()}} a final time and reports that files were
saved.\cr
}

Because the \code{save} event is scheduled from the \code{\link[=outputs]{outputs()}} table, an empty
table means no \code{save} events are scheduled at all. Populating that table
before calling \code{\link[=spades]{spades()}} is what turns saving on.
}

